3.2 lm-evaluation-harness — LLM standardized test engine
Repository — https://github.com/EleutherAI/lm-evaluation-harness [MIT]
Use it for standardized LLM benchmarks and as the main reference implementation for custom LLM task definitions. The project is active and its package metadata declares MIT licensing; the project exposes a CLI and supports local models and API-style model backends. [2]
Do not make benchmark percentages your final “knowledge score.” Use them as evidence for specific capability facets. A benchmark result becomes one observation in the Genome, not the Genome itself.
3.3 DeepEval — application-level and RAG evaluator
Repository — https://github.com/confident-ai/deepeval [Apache-2.0]
Use DeepEval for test-case-centric application evaluation, including LLM-as-a-judge style measurements and RAG-oriented tests. It complements, rather than replaces, deterministic evaluation. [3]
Design rule: every judge-based metric must include evaluator version, judge model version, judge prompt hash, and calibration set. Otherwise the metric cannot be trusted for longitudinal comparisons.
4. The Model Genome
The core proprietary value should live in your own schema, not in a third-party framework. Each model version receives a Genome containing evidence-backed estimates for knowledge, capability, reliability, calibration, cost, latency, resource requirements, failure modes, and augmentation response.
ModelGenome
  model_id
  provider_id
  model_version
  adapter_version
  modality
  context_limits
  languages

  facets[]
    facet_id
    domain
    capability
    state: KNOWN|PARTIAL|UNKNOWN|OUTDATED|CONTRADICTORY|UNCERTAIN
    score
    calibrated_confidence
    sample_count
    last_verified_at
    drift_score
    intrinsic_score
    augmented_score
    augmentation_gain
    hallucination_rate
    failure_modes[]
    evidence_refs[]

  resource_profile
    vram_mb / ram_mb / cpu_threads
    tokens_per_second / realtime_factor
    p50 / p95 latency
    cost_per_1k_tokens_or_unit
Use Bayesian or confidence-interval-aware estimates internally. A raw 0.87 without sample count and uncertainty bounds is not acceptable. The UI may show a clean percentage, but the underlying record must retain enough evidence to explain it.
5. Massive test generation without wasting compute
5.1 Active knowledge discovery
Avoid one-agent-per-question. That architecture wastes tokens and makes millions of tests expensive. Instead use a deterministic test executor plus small generator/evaluator workers. The generator should create variants only where uncertainty, contradiction, drift, or poor calibration is detected.
Seed facts / tasks
      |
      v
Test Generator
  |  paraphrase / adversarial / inverse / multi-hop /
  |  temporal / multilingual / synthetic-world variants
      v
Deduplicator + Coverage Planner
      v
Priority Queue
      |
      +--> CPU deterministic tests
      +--> small-model judge
      +--> heavy judge only for ambiguous cases
      v
Evidence Store
      v
Active Learner selects next frontier
The key objective is information gain per unit of compute, not raw test count. If 100,000 questions provide no new coverage, they are worse than 1,000 well-targeted frontier tests.
5.2 Synthetic knowledge islands
Build private synthetic worlds that the model could not have memorized. Give the model controlled facts about fictional entities, then test recall, compositional reasoning, contradiction handling, and use of retrieved context. Keep the test corpus private to reduce benchmark contamination and re-generate portions periodically.
5.3 Negative and abstention tests
Every knowledge facet needs false-premise, missing-evidence, contradiction, and impossible-query tests. A system that maximizes answer rate can be worse than a system that answers only when evidence is sufficient.
6. Brain / RAG stack
6.1 PostgreSQL + Apache AGE for durable relational + graph truth
Apache AGE — https://github.com/apache/age [Apache-2.0]
PostgreSQL — https://www.postgresql.org/ [PostgreSQL License]
Use PostgreSQL as the durable system of record for models, tests, evidence, provenance, versions, metrics, users, and audit trails. AGE adds graph functionality as a PostgreSQL extension, which makes graph relationships queryable without introducing a separate distributed graph system. [6]
This is deliberately chosen over a more specialized graph-only database because your system already has strong relational requirements and you want low operational overhead. A separate graph engine can be introduced later if profiling proves it necessary.
6.2 Qdrant for semantic/hybrid retrieval
Repository — https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant [Apache-2.0]
Use Qdrant for dense, sparse, multivector retrieval and payload filtering. Its current Query API supports parallel prefetch and fusion patterns for hybrid search, which fits the “exact term + semantic meaning” requirement of technical knowledge. [7]
Store chunk embeddings, entity embeddings, claim embeddings, and optionally separate embeddings for summaries. Avoid an explosion of named vectors: only create representations that correspond to a real retrieval need.
6.3 Tantivy for cheap lexical search
Repository — https://github.com/quickwit-oss/tantivy [MIT]
Use Tantivy as a local or embedded BM25/full-text component for exact identifiers, version strings, API names, package names, file paths, and code symbols. It is a Rust library and is considerably lighter than a full Elasticsearch-style stack. [8]
