7. Inference and worker layer
7.1 llama.cpp as the low-resource baseline
Repository — https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp [MIT]
Use llama.cpp as the default local execution path for small and quantized LLM workers. It minimizes the footprint of the worker node and is suitable for CPU-heavy or modest-GPU environments. [9]
7.2 vLLM / SGLang for high-throughput GPU workers
vLLM — https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm [Apache-2.0]
SGLang — https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang [Apache-2.0]
Run one of these only on workers where throughput justifies the additional memory and runtime complexity. The control plane should treat them as interchangeable serving adapters through an OpenAI-compatible internal contract. Do not hardwire application logic to vLLM or SGLang APIs.
7.3 LiteLLM as provider gateway — optional but valuable
Repository — https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm [Review current repository licensing before distribution; project advertises open-source gateway functionality]
Use LiteLLM at the provider boundary when you need one OpenAI-style interface across remote LLM APIs and local endpoints. Its current repository advertises support for 100+ LLM providers and endpoints including embeddings, images, audio, batches and rerank. [28]
Strict permissive-only rule: perform an automated license audit in CI before each release. Keep LiteLLM as a replaceable gateway, not an irreducible dependency of the core cognitive fabric.
