9. Data and storage
9.1 DuckDB + Arrow
DuckDB — https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb [MIT]
Apache Arrow — https://github.com/apache/arrow [Apache-2.0]
Use Arrow for portable columnar result batches and DuckDB for analytical queries over millions/billions of observations without forcing every query through PostgreSQL. Store raw result shards as Parquet. Compact only after checksums and provenance are recorded.
9.2 Apache OpenDAL
Repository — https://github.com/apache/opendal [Apache-2.0]
Use OpenDAL as the storage abstraction between the brain/evaluation services and local filesystem, S3-compatible storage, or other object stores. This keeps the application independent from a specific storage vendor and follows its object-storage-first design. [24]
10. Multimodal evaluation adapters
10.1 Speech-to-text and speech understanding
OpenAI Whisper — https://github.com/openai/whisper [MIT]
faster-whisper — https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper [MIT]
WhisperX — https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX [BSD-2-Clause]
Use an independent ASR stack to validate generated speech and to create reference transcripts for speech tests. Whisper’s model card explicitly notes that the model can hallucinate text that is not present in the audio, so ASR output must itself be treated as evidence with confidence and cross-checking, not as ground truth. [12][13][14]
10.2 TTS verification
ViSQOL — https://github.com/google/visqol [Apache-2.0]
SpeechBrain — https://github.com/speechbrain/speechbrain [Apache-2.0]
librosa — https://github.com/librosa/librosa [ISC]
For TTS, combine transcript intelligibility (ASR), acoustic measurements, reference-based quality where a reference exists, speaker similarity when authorized reference audio exists, and human preference samples. ViSQOL is a full-reference metric and explicitly warns that it can behave poorly outside its intended codec/VoIP-style domain, so it should be one verifier among several. [15]
10.3 Image generation and vision-language evaluation
VLMEvalKit — https://github.com/open-compass/VLMEvalKit [Apache-2.0]
OpenCLIP — https://github.com/mlfoundations/open_clip [MIT]
Hugging Face Diffusers — https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers [Apache-2.0]
For image generators, separate prompt adherence, object presence, spatial relations, text rendering, aesthetic quality, and identity consistency. Use VLM-based verification for semantics, OpenCLIP-like similarity as a supporting signal, and deterministic computer-vision checks where possible. Diffusers is the recommended permissive framework layer for integrating diffusion pipelines; individual model weights may have their own licenses and must be audited separately. [17][30]
10.4 Video generation
VideoScore — https://github.com/TIGER-AI-Lab/VideoScore [Review repository/data/model license before redistribution]
GenAI-Bench — https://github.com/TIGER-AI-Lab/GenAI-Bench [Review repository/data license before redistribution]
Wan2.2 — https://github.com/Wan-Video/Wan2.2 [Apache-2.0 code repository; audit weights separately]
For video, use a verifier stack: frame-level prompt alignment, temporal consistency, motion quality, identity continuity, camera/scene constraints, and audio-video synchronization. VideoScore and GenAI-Bench are useful research components, but they should not be treated as the sole source of truth. [18][19][31]
Important licensing boundary: the fact that a repository contains Apache-2.0 code does not automatically mean every model weight, dataset, checkpoint, or generated data asset inside the ecosystem has the same license. CI must audit code, model, data, and third-party dependency licenses independently.
11. Cognitive router
The Router consumes Genome + task requirements + knowledge state + resource policy. It does not merely rank models. It chooses a strategy.
Task
  -> classify intent / modality / risk / freshness need
  -> find required capabilities
  -> lookup Model Genome
  -> estimate intrinsic success probability
  -> estimate augmentation gain from Brain
  -> estimate tool gain
  -> estimate cost / latency / resource use
  -> choose strategy

Strategies:
  MODEL_ONLY
  MODEL_PLUS_BRAIN
  SEARCH_PLUS_MODEL
  TOOL_PLUS_MODEL
  SMALL_MODEL_PLUS_VERIFIER
  MODEL_A_CRITIC_MODEL_B
  MODEL_A -> TOOL -> MODEL_A -> VERIFY
Decision rule: only invoke the brain when expected utility increases enough to justify retrieval and context cost. This requires empirical learning from prior traces, not a hand-written “always RAG” rule.
12. Truth, provenance and calibration
The brain must distinguish between a fact, a model claim, an inference, a hypothesis, and an unknown. Every claim is attached to sources, timestamps, version identifiers, and verification history.
Claim
  claim_id
  subject / predicate / object
  status: FACT|MODEL_CLAIM|INFERENCE|HYPOTHESIS|UNKNOWN
  valid_from / valid_until / observed_at
  source_refs[]
  source_reliability
  verification_runs[]
  contradictions[]
  confidence_interval
  checksum / content_hash
Calibration is mandatory. For example, if the UI says a facet is 90% likely to be answered correctly, historical predictions in that probability bucket should actually be near 90% correct. Store calibration curves and Brier-style or equivalent calibration metrics.
13. Scaling to millions and beyond
The architecture scales by separating the cheap coordination plane from expensive evaluation workers. The coordinator should never hold all raw prompts or outputs in RAM. Tests are immutable work items with content-addressed IDs; workers fetch inputs, execute, stream compact results, and release local artifacts.
Scale
Recommended behavior
Main resource
10k tests
Single controller + a few workers
CPU/GPU as needed
1M tests
NATS + worker pools + Parquet + DuckDB compaction
Network + inference
10M+ tests
Partition by model/version/facet/seed; checkpoint campaigns
Object storage + compute
100M+ tests
Active learning + frontier targeting; avoid uniform brute force
Compute efficiency is the bottleneck
Long-running campaigns
Temporal workflows + immutable artifacts
Reliability / recovery

Do not promise “hundreds of millions of unique knowledge discoveries.” The objective is high coverage with low redundancy. Test amplification and active learning should concentrate compute around uncertainty and failure boundaries.
14. Low-resource engineering rules
Prefer Go or Rust for hot-path services: event gateway, worker agent, artifact transfer, indexing glue, and local collectors.
Prefer Python only where model libraries require it: evaluation adapters, dataset logic, judges, and ML-specific metrics.
Use llama.cpp for small local LLM judges and classifiers; reserve heavyweight GPU serving for throughput-critical pools.
Keep the controller stateless where possible; persist state in PostgreSQL and artifacts in Parquet/object storage.
Batch embeddings, ASR, VLM and judge requests. Avoid one process/container per item.
Use deterministic rules before an LLM judge. Escalate only ambiguous cases.
Use content hashes and deduplication before running an expensive test.
Cache model responses only when the cache key includes model/version/configuration/prompt/context/tool state.
Prefer one PostgreSQL deployment plus Qdrant over a large collection of overlapping databases until scale measurements prove otherwise.
Audit all third-party licenses in CI, including model weights and datasets, not just code repositories.
