18. 10/10 acceptance gates
Gate
Required condition
Reproducibility
Same test + same model version + same config produces traceable rerun metadata.
Evidence
Every Genome score links to raw/normalized evidence and evaluator version.
Calibration
Confidence is empirically checked, not decorative.
No leakage
Private holdouts and synthetic-world tests exist.
Active discovery
System targets uncertain/weak facets rather than brute-force only.
RAG causality
Improvement from retrieval is measured against a control condition.
Multimodal independence
Each modality uses appropriate metrics and cross-checks.
Cost awareness
Router tracks quality, latency and compute/token cost.
Regression safety
Model updates can trigger facet-level regression alerts.
Licensing
Code, weights, datasets and dependencies are independently audited.
Failure transparency
UNKNOWN and UNCERTAIN are first-class states; the system can abstain.
Scalability
Campaigns resume after failures and do not require all results in RAM.

19. Projects deliberately not used as core dependencies
Some popular projects are excellent technically but conflict with the strict “open + permissive + commercial-friendly” requirement or add avoidable operational complexity.
Meilisearch: its current repository is dual-licensed at the repository level, with Enterprise Edition components under BSL-1.1 while the community edition remains MIT. For a strict permissive-only baseline, use Tantivy instead and add Meilisearch only if the exact needed CE surface is verified. [32][33]
FalkorDB: technically attractive for GraphRAG, but the server is currently SSPL-1.0, so it is not suitable as the strict permissive core. [34]
Langfuse: useful, but only the core is MIT; EE directories are separately licensed. Keep it optional or isolate core-only use under license policy. [25][35]
Silero model repository: its main model licensing is not permissive-only; the repository states CC-NC-BY for many models, with exceptions. Do not make those model assets part of a permissive baseline. [36]
Stanford HELM: excellent research reference, but it entered maintenance mode on June 1, 2026. Use it as a reference, not as the main future execution layer. [37]
